Replace the destination of a sequence copy in one KV cache stream
Fold-into: upstream/memory

The defects. tests/fuzz/core/run.sh test replays the reproducer of each one.

- A copy into a sequence that holds cells keeps its old cells in the KV cache.

  llama_kv_cache::seq_cp between two sequences of one stream (kv_unified = true) adds the
  destination to each cell of the source in [p0, p1), and it does not remove the old cells of the
  destination in that range. The destination then holds two cells at one position, and its
  attention reads the old tokens next to the copied ones. A second copy into the same
  destination stops a debug build on the assert of llama_kv_cells::seq_add, because a cell holds
  the destination from the first copy. In a hybrid model the recurrent memory replaces the
  destination (it gives the destination the cell of the source), thus the two memories of one
  sequence disagree. On the 2B model the next decode of the destination differs from a decode of
  its history by 2.25 in a logit.

  The semantics: a copy replaces the destination in [p0, p1). The other copy paths of the memory
  modules do this: the copy between two streams (kv_unified = false) resets the stream of the
  destination, and the recurrent memory drops the old cell of the destination. A refusal is not
  possible, because seq_cp returns no status. Each caller in upstream (llama-server, the
  speculative, lookahead and parallel examples) empties the destination before the copy or
  copies into an unused sequence, thus its result does not change. The app does not copy
  sequences.

  The change: The same-stream path removes the destination from the cells in [p0, p1) with
  seq_rm, then adds it to the cells of the source. The next decode of the destination is then
  bitwise equal to a decode of its history (the tiny model and the 2B, kv_unified 1), and the
  result of kv_unified = true agrees with that of kv_unified = false.

  The reproducer: tests/fuzz/core/regress/fuzz_recurrent--copy-into-used-sequence.

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 with the patches of patches/series above this one.

Files:
    src/llama-kv-cache.cpp
--- a/src/llama-kv-cache.cpp
+++ b/src/llama-kv-cache.cpp
@@ -470,6 +470,15 @@ void llama_kv_cache::seq_cp(llama_seq_id seq_id_src, llama_seq_id seq_id_dst, ll
             return;
         }
 
+        // The copy replaces the destination in [p0, p1), as the copy between two streams (which
+        // resets the stream of the destination) and the recurrent memory (which gives the
+        // destination the cell of the source) do. Without this remove, a destination that holds
+        // cells in the range keeps them next to the cells of the source: two cells at one position
+        // of one sequence, which its attention reads, or an assert in seq_add for a cell that the
+        // two sequences share from an earlier copy. The callers of upstream remove the destination
+        // first (llama-server, the speculative examples), thus their result does not change.
+        seq_rm(seq_id_dst, p0, p1);
+
         if (p0 < 0) {
             p0 = 0;
         }
